// Fixture: string_escape_lone_trailing_backslash
// NOT strictly requested -- added because implementing the parity fix
// surfaced a genuinely non-obvious result worth golden-locking rather
// than only describing in prose.
//
// A single unpaired backslash immediately before what looks like a
// string's closing quote (e.g. `"abc\"`, ONE backslash, not the
// paired-and-complete `\\` two backslashes tested in
// string_escaped_trailing_backslash.gddl) is NOT rejected by the
// phase-6 "string_escape" check at all. Per the parity rule, an odd
// backslash count immediately before a `"` means that `"` IS escaped
// (a literal quote character, not a boundary) -- so the tokenizer
// correctly keeps scanning for a LATER, real closing quote. If none
// exists before end of line/file, the result is "unterminated string
// literal" at phase 3 (parsing), never reaching phase 6's unescape
// logic at all.
//
// Both this and string_escape_invalid_sequence.gddl satisfy the spec's
// "a lone `\` at the end of a string... is rejected outright" language
// -- they're just two DIFFERENT rejection mechanisms (phase 3 tokenizer
// boundary detection vs. phase 6 escape-sequence validation), depending
// on whether the malformed backslash appears at the very end of the
// literal (where it's ambiguous with the closing quote itself) or
// mid-string (where the tokenizer has already found a real, unambiguous
// terminator and phase 6 gets to inspect the escape sequences within).

define Message
	text = string 40

Message Unterminated
	text = "trailing backslash\"

// Expected:
//   Whole-file parse error (phase 3), NOT a per-instance phase-6
//   error -- this fixture has no "instances" key in its golden output,
//   matching the shape every other whole-file parse failure in this
//   corpus uses. check: "unterminated string literal". The single `\`
//   before the apparent closing quote is interpreted as escaping that
//   quote (per the parity rule), so the tokenizer never finds a real
//   terminator on this line and correctly reports the string as
//   unterminated.
